# Findings gitleaks has raised that are not secrets.
#
# FINGERPRINTS, not paths or rules. Each line pins the exemption to one commit,
# one file, one rule and one line, so a genuine secret on the next line -- or the
# same line under a different rule -- still fails the build. Silencing the rule
# or the file would turn a scan of this repository into a scan of nothing much,
# and migrations/001_init_schema.sql once carried working site keys, which is the
# reason the secret scan exists at all.
#
# Why an ignore file and not a `//gitleaks:allow` comment: on a pull request the
# action scans base..head, so the finding lives in the PATCH OF THE COMMIT that
# introduced it. Annotating the file today writes the comment into a NEW commit
# and leaves the old diff exactly as it was -- the finding would keep firing, and
# it would look like the fix simply had not worked.

